# tts — an impure-boundary PORT for text-to-speech, declared once and used by any
# domain that speaks (Voice). Two-color rule : sync domain, async adapter, NEVER a
# lock — every async concern lives at the adapter edge, never in the synchronous core.
#
# Converted from the dead adapters/tts/tts.family (parser-removal plan, Phase
# 1a — `.family` was never a parsed hecksagain construct). Lives at the
# repo-root ports/ directory — see messaging.port's own comment for why.
#
# Signal is EFFECT, FIRE-AND-FORGET : the aggregate EMITS a speak event, the adapter
# renders + plays the audio OUT-OF-PROCESS, and NOTHING re-enters the domain
# (speech is heard, not awaited). Unlike payment/llm — an effect WITH a verdict that
# round-trips — tts has no success/failure re-entry : there is no `produces`, and the
# bind carries no `do success/failure end` block. The domain announces what to say
# and moves on.
#
# The how-verb is `voiced_by`, hung off the aggregate FQN with its triggering event
# (`on:`). Per-deployment config (voice_id, model, speed, stability,
# similarity_boost, style, cache_dir, auto_play) lives on the adapter that
# implements this port and in .world — a port names verb/signal only here, no
# config fields. The API key is NOT a field — the handler reads it from
# ~/.config/miette/elevenlabs.key, a file that never appears as a bluebook value.
Hecks.port "tts" do
  verb   "voiced_by"
  signal :effect
end
